<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><channel><title>GoConstant - Tag - loommii</title><link>https://loommii.github.io/en/tags/goconstant/</link><description>我的个人技术博客</description><generator>Hugo 0.165.0 &amp; FixIt v0.4.5</generator><language>en</language><lastBuildDate>Tue, 08 Sep 2026 18:13:39 +0800</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://loommii.github.io/en/tags/goconstant/index.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><title>Go Daily Question 223</title><link>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_223/</link><pubDate>Sun, 04 May 2025 15:50:53 +0800</pubDate><guid>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_223/</guid><category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/godailyquestion/">GoDailyQuestion</category><category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/golanguagebasics/">GoLanguageBasics</category><description>&lt;p&gt;Consider the following 3 lines of code:&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;// 32-bit machine&#10;1）var x int32 = 32.0&#10;2）var y int = x&#10;3）var z rune = x&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;p&gt;Will they compile? Why?&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;If asked this question in an interview, you need to consider what the interviewer wants to test you on.&#10;&#10;&lt;details&gt;&#10; &lt;summary&gt;🔑 Answer Analysis:&lt;/summary&gt;&#10; &lt;h1 class="heading-element" id="numeric-literals"&gt;&lt;span&gt;Numeric Literals&lt;/span&gt;&#10; &lt;a href="#numeric-literals" class="heading-mark"&gt;&#10; &lt;svg class="octicon octicon-link" viewBox="0 0 16 16" version="1.1" width="16" height="16" aria-hidden="true"&gt;&lt;path d="m7.775 3.275 1.25-1.25a3.5 3.5 0 1 1 4.95 4.95l-2.5 2.5a3.5 3.5 0 0 1-4.95 0 .751.751 0 0 1 .018-1.042.751.751 0 0 1 1.042-.018 1.998 1.998 0 0 0 2.83 0l2.5-2.5a2.002 2.002 0 0 0-2.83-2.83l-1.25 1.25a.751.751 0 0 1-1.042-.018.751.751 0 0 1-.018-1.042Zm-4.69 9.64a1.998 1.998 0 0 0 2.83 0l1.25-1.25a.751.751 0 0 1 1.042.018.751.751 0 0 1 .018 1.042l-1.25 1.25a3.5 3.5 0 1 1-4.95-4.95l2.5-2.5a3.5 3.5 0 0 1 4.95 0 .751.751 0 0 1-.018 1.042.751.751 0 0 1-1.042.018 1.998 1.998 0 0 0-2.83 0l-2.5 2.5a1.998 1.998 0 0 0 0 2.83Z"&gt;&lt;/path&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&#10; &lt;/a&gt;&#10;&lt;/h1&gt;&lt;p&gt;In Go, literals are untyped. What does untyped mean? Untyped means they can be assigned to variables or constants of similar types. In the example above, &lt;code&gt;32.0&lt;/code&gt; is an untyped floating-point literal, so it can be assigned to any numeric type variable (or constant). All of the following are legal:&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Go Daily Question 211</title><link>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_211/</link><pubDate>Wed, 12 Mar 2025 01:16:28 +0800</pubDate><guid>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_211/</guid><category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/godailyquestion/">GoDailyQuestion</category><category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/golanguagebasics/">GoLanguageBasics</category><description>&lt;p&gt;Go101 author published an interesting problem that primarily tests subtle details, a characteristic for which the book Go101 is well-known. Let&amp;rsquo;s look at this problem: what is the output of the following program? (Single choice)&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;package main&#10;&#10;const s = &amp;#34;Go101.org&amp;#34;&#10;&#10;// len(s) == 9&#10;// 1 &amp;lt;&amp;lt; 9 == 512&#10;// 512 / 128 == 4&#10;&#10;var a byte = 1 &amp;lt;&amp;lt; len(s) / 128&#10;var b byte = 1 &amp;lt;&amp;lt; len(s[:]) / 128&#10;&#10;func main() {&#10; println(a, b)&#10;}&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;ul&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;A、0 0&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;B、0 4&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;C：4 0&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;D：4 4&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;/ul&gt;&#10;&#10;&lt;details&gt;&#10; &lt;summary&gt;🔑 Answer Analysis:&lt;/summary&gt;&#10; &lt;p&gt;There are 2 key points here:&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Go Daily Question 175</title><link>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_175/</link><pubDate>Tue, 28 Jan 2025 10:15:27 +0800</pubDate><guid>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_175/</guid><category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/godailyquestion/">GoDailyQuestion</category><category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/golanguagebasics/">GoLanguageBasics</category><description>&lt;p&gt;What is the output of the following code?&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;package main&#10;&#10;import &amp;#34;fmt&amp;#34;&#10;&#10;const (&#10; a = iota&#10; b = iota&#10;)&#10;const (&#10; name = &amp;#34;name&amp;#34;&#10; c = iota&#10; d = iota&#10;)&#10;&#10;func main() {&#10; fmt.Println(a)&#10; fmt.Println(b)&#10; fmt.Println(c)&#10; fmt.Println(d)&#10;}&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#10;&lt;details&gt;&#10; &lt;summary&gt;🔑 Answer Analysis:&lt;/summary&gt;&#10; &lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;0&#10;1&#10;1&#10;2&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://go.dev/play/p/3owhzDo5QeG" target="_blank" rel="external nofollow noopener noreferrer"&gt;Run online&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Code Analysis:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;h3 class="heading-element" id="first-const-block"&gt;&lt;span&gt;First &lt;code&gt;const&lt;/code&gt; block&lt;/span&gt;&#10; &lt;a href="#first-const-block" class="heading-mark"&gt;&#10; &lt;svg class="octicon octicon-link" viewBox="0 0 16 16" version="1.1" width="16" height="16" aria-hidden="true"&gt;&lt;path d="m7.775 3.275 1.25-1.25a3.5 3.5 0 1 1 4.95 4.95l-2.5 2.5a3.5 3.5 0 0 1-4.95 0 .751.751 0 0 1 .018-1.042.751.751 0 0 1 1.042-.018 1.998 1.998 0 0 0 2.83 0l2.5-2.5a2.002 2.002 0 0 0-2.83-2.83l-1.25 1.25a.751.751 0 0 1-1.042-.018.751.751 0 0 1-.018-1.042Zm-4.69 9.64a1.998 1.998 0 0 0 2.83 0l1.25-1.25a.751.751 0 0 1 1.042.018.751.751 0 0 1 .018 1.042l-1.25 1.25a3.5 3.5 0 1 1-4.95-4.95l2.5-2.5a3.5 3.5 0 0 1 4.95 0 .751.751 0 0 1-.018 1.042.751.751 0 0 1-1.042.018 1.998 1.998 0 0 0-2.83 0l-2.5 2.5a1.998 1.998 0 0 0 0 2.83Z"&gt;&lt;/path&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&#10; &lt;/a&gt;&#10;&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;ul&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;&lt;code&gt;a = iota&lt;/code&gt;: &lt;code&gt;iota&lt;/code&gt; starts counting from &lt;code&gt;0&lt;/code&gt; in a &lt;code&gt;const&lt;/code&gt; block, so &lt;code&gt;a = 0&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;&lt;code&gt;b = iota&lt;/code&gt;: &lt;code&gt;iota&lt;/code&gt; increments to &lt;code&gt;1&lt;/code&gt;, so &lt;code&gt;b = 1&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;/ul&gt;&#10;&lt;h3 class="heading-element" id="second-const-block"&gt;&lt;span&gt;Second &lt;code&gt;const&lt;/code&gt; block&lt;/span&gt;&#10; &lt;a href="#second-const-block" class="heading-mark"&gt;&#10; &lt;svg class="octicon octicon-link" viewBox="0 0 16 16" version="1.1" width="16" height="16" aria-hidden="true"&gt;&lt;path d="m7.775 3.275 1.25-1.25a3.5 3.5 0 1 1 4.95 4.95l-2.5 2.5a3.5 3.5 0 0 1-4.95 0 .751.751 0 0 1 .018-1.042.751.751 0 0 1 1.042-.018 1.998 1.998 0 0 0 2.83 0l2.5-2.5a2.002 2.002 0 0 0-2.83-2.83l-1.25 1.25a.751.751 0 0 1-1.042-.018.751.751 0 0 1-.018-1.042Zm-4.69 9.64a1.998 1.998 0 0 0 2.83 0l1.25-1.25a.751.751 0 0 1 1.042.018.751.751 0 0 1 .018 1.042l-1.25 1.25a3.5 3.5 0 1 1-4.95-4.95l2.5-2.5a3.5 3.5 0 0 1 4.95 0 .751.751 0 0 1-.018 1.042.751.751 0 0 1-1.042.018 1.998 1.998 0 0 0-2.83 0l-2.5 2.5a1.998 1.998 0 0 0 0 2.83Z"&gt;&lt;/path&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&#10; &lt;/a&gt;&#10;&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;ul&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;&lt;code&gt;name = &amp;quot;name&amp;quot;&lt;/code&gt;: &lt;code&gt;iota&lt;/code&gt; is not used, but &lt;code&gt;iota&lt;/code&gt;&amp;rsquo;s initial value is &lt;code&gt;0&lt;/code&gt;, and it increments to &lt;code&gt;1&lt;/code&gt; after declaration.&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;&lt;code&gt;c = iota&lt;/code&gt;: The current &lt;code&gt;iota&lt;/code&gt; value is &lt;code&gt;1&lt;/code&gt;, so &lt;code&gt;c = 1&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;&lt;code&gt;d = iota&lt;/code&gt;: &lt;code&gt;iota&lt;/code&gt; continues to increment to &lt;code&gt;2&lt;/code&gt;, so &lt;code&gt;d = 2&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;/ul&gt;&#10;&lt;h3 class="heading-element" id="key-rules"&gt;&lt;span&gt;Key Rules&lt;/span&gt;&#10; &lt;a href="#key-rules" class="heading-mark"&gt;&#10; &lt;svg class="octicon octicon-link" viewBox="0 0 16 16" version="1.1" width="16" height="16" aria-hidden="true"&gt;&lt;path d="m7.775 3.275 1.25-1.25a3.5 3.5 0 1 1 4.95 4.95l-2.5 2.5a3.5 3.5 0 0 1-4.95 0 .751.751 0 0 1 .018-1.042.751.751 0 0 1 1.042-.018 1.998 1.998 0 0 0 2.83 0l2.5-2.5a2.002 2.002 0 0 0-2.83-2.83l-1.25 1.25a.751.751 0 0 1-1.042-.018.751.751 0 0 1-.018-1.042Zm-4.69 9.64a1.998 1.998 0 0 0 2.83 0l1.25-1.25a.751.751 0 0 1 1.042.018.751.751 0 0 1 .018 1.042l-1.25 1.25a3.5 3.5 0 1 1-4.95-4.95l2.5-2.5a3.5 3.5 0 0 1 4.95 0 .751.751 0 0 1-.018 1.042.751.751 0 0 1-1.042.018 1.998 1.998 0 0 0-2.83 0l-2.5 2.5a1.998 1.998 0 0 0 0 2.83Z"&gt;&lt;/path&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&#10; &lt;/a&gt;&#10;&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;ul&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;&lt;code&gt;iota&lt;/code&gt; starts counting from &lt;code&gt;0&lt;/code&gt; in each &lt;code&gt;const&lt;/code&gt; block.&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;Each line of constant declaration (whether &lt;code&gt;iota&lt;/code&gt; is used or not) increments &lt;code&gt;iota&lt;/code&gt; by &lt;code&gt;1&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;&lt;code&gt;iota&lt;/code&gt; in different &lt;code&gt;const&lt;/code&gt; blocks are independent of each other.&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;/ul&gt;&#10;&lt;/details&gt;</description></item><item><title>Go Daily Question 171</title><link>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_171/</link><pubDate>Sat, 25 Jan 2025 23:19:23 +0800</pubDate><guid>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_171/</guid><category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/godailyquestion/">GoDailyQuestion</category><category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/golanguagebasics/">GoLanguageBasics</category><description>&lt;p&gt;What is the output of the following code?&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;package main&#10;&#10;import (&#10; &amp;#34;fmt&amp;#34;&#10;)&#10;&#10;func main() {&#10; var ans float64 = 15 &amp;#43; 25 &amp;#43; 5.2&#10; fmt.Println(ans)&#10;}&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;ul&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;A: Cannot compile;&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;B: 45;&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;C: 45.2;&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;D: 45.0&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;/ul&gt;&#10;&#10;&lt;details&gt;&#10; &lt;summary&gt;🔑 Answer Analysis:&lt;/summary&gt;&#10; &lt;p&gt;C&#10;&lt;a href="https://go.dev/play/p/VCR1yAY32nG" target="_blank" rel="external nofollow noopener noreferrer"&gt;Run online&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;h2 class="heading-element" id="analysis"&gt;&lt;span&gt;Analysis&lt;/span&gt;&#10; &lt;a href="#analysis" class="heading-mark"&gt;&#10; &lt;svg class="octicon octicon-link" viewBox="0 0 16 16" version="1.1" width="16" height="16" aria-hidden="true"&gt;&lt;path d="m7.775 3.275 1.25-1.25a3.5 3.5 0 1 1 4.95 4.95l-2.5 2.5a3.5 3.5 0 0 1-4.95 0 .751.751 0 0 1 .018-1.042.751.751 0 0 1 1.042-.018 1.998 1.998 0 0 0 2.83 0l2.5-2.5a2.002 2.002 0 0 0-2.83-2.83l-1.25 1.25a.751.751 0 0 1-1.042-.018.751.751 0 0 1-.018-1.042Zm-4.69 9.64a1.998 1.998 0 0 0 2.83 0l1.25-1.25a.751.751 0 0 1 1.042.018.751.751 0 0 1 .018 1.042l-1.25 1.25a3.5 3.5 0 1 1-4.95-4.95l2.5-2.5a3.5 3.5 0 0 1 4.95 0 .751.751 0 0 1-.018 1.042.751.751 0 0 1-1.042.018 1.998 1.998 0 0 0-2.83 0l-2.5 2.5a1.998 1.998 0 0 0 0 2.83Z"&gt;&lt;/path&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&#10; &lt;/a&gt;&#10;&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;h3 class="heading-element" id="1-constant-expressions"&gt;&lt;span&gt;1. Constant Expressions&lt;/span&gt;&#10; &lt;a href="#1-constant-expressions" class="heading-mark"&gt;&#10; &lt;svg class="octicon octicon-link" viewBox="0 0 16 16" version="1.1" width="16" height="16" aria-hidden="true"&gt;&lt;path d="m7.775 3.275 1.25-1.25a3.5 3.5 0 1 1 4.95 4.95l-2.5 2.5a3.5 3.5 0 0 1-4.95 0 .751.751 0 0 1 .018-1.042.751.751 0 0 1 1.042-.018 1.998 1.998 0 0 0 2.83 0l2.5-2.5a2.002 2.002 0 0 0-2.83-2.83l-1.25 1.25a.751.751 0 0 1-1.042-.018.751.751 0 0 1-.018-1.042Zm-4.69 9.64a1.998 1.998 0 0 0 2.83 0l1.25-1.25a.751.751 0 0 1 1.042.018.751.751 0 0 1 .018 1.042l-1.25 1.25a3.5 3.5 0 1 1-4.95-4.95l2.5-2.5a3.5 3.5 0 0 1 4.95 0 .751.751 0 0 1-.018 1.042.751.751 0 0 1-1.042.018 1.998 1.998 0 0 0-2.83 0l-2.5 2.5a1.998 1.998 0 0 0 0 2.83Z"&gt;&lt;/path&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&#10; &lt;/a&gt;&#10;&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;ul&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;A &lt;strong&gt;constant expression&lt;/strong&gt; is an expression that contains only constant operands and is evaluated at compile time.&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;Constants can be:&#10;&lt;ul&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Untyped constants&lt;/strong&gt;: e.g., &lt;code&gt;1&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;2&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;5.2&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Typed constants&lt;/strong&gt;: e.g., &lt;code&gt;const b int8 = 3&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Literal constants&lt;/strong&gt;: e.g., &lt;code&gt;1&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;2&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;5.2&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Named constants&lt;/strong&gt;: e.g., &lt;code&gt;const a = 1 + 2&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;/ul&gt;&#10;&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;/ul&gt;&#10;&lt;h3 class="heading-element" id="2-default-types-of-untyped-constants"&gt;&lt;span&gt;2. Default Types of Untyped Constants&lt;/span&gt;&#10; &lt;a href="#2-default-types-of-untyped-constants" class="heading-mark"&gt;&#10; &lt;svg class="octicon octicon-link" viewBox="0 0 16 16" version="1.1" width="16" height="16" aria-hidden="true"&gt;&lt;path d="m7.775 3.275 1.25-1.25a3.5 3.5 0 1 1 4.95 4.95l-2.5 2.5a3.5 3.5 0 0 1-4.95 0 .751.751 0 0 1 .018-1.042.751.751 0 0 1 1.042-.018 1.998 1.998 0 0 0 2.83 0l2.5-2.5a2.002 2.002 0 0 0-2.83-2.83l-1.25 1.25a.751.751 0 0 1-1.042-.018.751.751 0 0 1-.018-1.042Zm-4.69 9.64a1.998 1.998 0 0 0 2.83 0l1.25-1.25a.751.751 0 0 1 1.042.018.751.751 0 0 1 .018 1.042l-1.25 1.25a3.5 3.5 0 1 1-4.95-4.95l2.5-2.5a3.5 3.5 0 0 1 4.95 0 .751.751 0 0 1-.018 1.042.751.751 0 0 1-1.042.018 1.998 1.998 0 0 0-2.83 0l-2.5 2.5a1.998 1.998 0 0 0 0 2.83Z"&gt;&lt;/path&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&#10; &lt;/a&gt;&#10;&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;ul&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;Although untyped constants do not have an explicit type, they have a default type:&#10;&lt;ul&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;Integer literals (e.g., &lt;code&gt;15&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;25&lt;/code&gt;) have a default type of &lt;code&gt;int&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;Floating-point literals (e.g., &lt;code&gt;5.2&lt;/code&gt;) have a default type of &lt;code&gt;float64&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;String literals have a default type of &lt;code&gt;string&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;Boolean literals have a default type of &lt;code&gt;bool&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;Complex literals have a default type of &lt;code&gt;complex128&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;/ul&gt;&#10;&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;/ul&gt;&#10;&lt;h3 class="heading-element" id="3-type-inference"&gt;&lt;span&gt;3. Type Inference&lt;/span&gt;&#10; &lt;a href="#3-type-inference" class="heading-mark"&gt;&#10; &lt;svg class="octicon octicon-link" viewBox="0 0 16 16" version="1.1" width="16" height="16" aria-hidden="true"&gt;&lt;path d="m7.775 3.275 1.25-1.25a3.5 3.5 0 1 1 4.95 4.95l-2.5 2.5a3.5 3.5 0 0 1-4.95 0 .751.751 0 0 1 .018-1.042.751.751 0 0 1 1.042-.018 1.998 1.998 0 0 0 2.83 0l2.5-2.5a2.002 2.002 0 0 0-2.83-2.83l-1.25 1.25a.751.751 0 0 1-1.042-.018.751.751 0 0 1-.018-1.042Zm-4.69 9.64a1.998 1.998 0 0 0 2.83 0l1.25-1.25a.751.751 0 0 1 1.042.018.751.751 0 0 1 .018 1.042l-1.25 1.25a3.5 3.5 0 1 1-4.95-4.95l2.5-2.5a3.5 3.5 0 0 1 4.95 0 .751.751 0 0 1-.018 1.042.751.751 0 0 1-1.042.018 1.998 1.998 0 0 0-2.83 0l-2.5 2.5a1.998 1.998 0 0 0 0 2.83Z"&gt;&lt;/path&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&#10; &lt;/a&gt;&#10;&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;ul&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;Go language supports type inference. If the context does not specify an explicit type, the compiler will treat untyped constants as their default type.&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;In a constant expression, if the operands include untyped constants of different types, the result&amp;rsquo;s type will be inferred according to the following precedence:&#10;&lt;ul&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;Integer &amp;lt; rune &amp;lt; float &amp;lt; complex.&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;/ul&gt;&#10;&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;/ul&gt;&#10;&lt;h3 class="heading-element" id="4-constant-expression-in-the-problem"&gt;&lt;span&gt;4. Constant Expression in the Problem&lt;/span&gt;&#10; &lt;a href="#4-constant-expression-in-the-problem" class="heading-mark"&gt;&#10; &lt;svg class="octicon octicon-link" viewBox="0 0 16 16" version="1.1" width="16" height="16" aria-hidden="true"&gt;&lt;path d="m7.775 3.275 1.25-1.25a3.5 3.5 0 1 1 4.95 4.95l-2.5 2.5a3.5 3.5 0 0 1-4.95 0 .751.751 0 0 1 .018-1.042.751.751 0 0 1 1.042-.018 1.998 1.998 0 0 0 2.83 0l2.5-2.5a2.002 2.002 0 0 0-2.83-2.83l-1.25 1.25a.751.751 0 0 1-1.042-.018.751.751 0 0 1-.018-1.042Zm-4.69 9.64a1.998 1.998 0 0 0 2.83 0l1.25-1.25a.751.751 0 0 1 1.042.018.751.751 0 0 1 .018 1.042l-1.25 1.25a3.5 3.5 0 1 1-4.95-4.95l2.5-2.5a3.5 3.5 0 0 1 4.95 0 .751.751 0 0 1-.018 1.042.751.751 0 0 1-1.042.018 1.998 1.998 0 0 0-2.83 0l-2.5 2.5a1.998 1.998 0 0 0 0 2.83Z"&gt;&lt;/path&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&#10; &lt;/a&gt;&#10;&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;var ans float64 = 15 &amp;#43; 25 &amp;#43; 5.2&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;ul&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;&lt;code&gt;15&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;25&lt;/code&gt; are untyped integer constants, with a default type of &lt;code&gt;int&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;&lt;code&gt;5.2&lt;/code&gt; is an untyped floating-point constant, with a default type of &lt;code&gt;float64&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;In the constant expression, since &lt;code&gt;5.2&lt;/code&gt; is a floating-point constant, the entire expression&amp;rsquo;s result will be inferred as a floating-point type (&lt;code&gt;float64&lt;/code&gt;).&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;/ul&gt;&#10;&lt;h3 class="heading-element" id="operation-process"&gt;&lt;span&gt;Operation Process&lt;/span&gt;&#10; &lt;a href="#operation-process" class="heading-mark"&gt;&#10; &lt;svg class="octicon octicon-link" viewBox="0 0 16 16" version="1.1" width="16" height="16" aria-hidden="true"&gt;&lt;path d="m7.775 3.275 1.25-1.25a3.5 3.5 0 1 1 4.95 4.95l-2.5 2.5a3.5 3.5 0 0 1-4.95 0 .751.751 0 0 1 .018-1.042.751.751 0 0 1 1.042-.018 1.998 1.998 0 0 0 2.83 0l2.5-2.5a2.002 2.002 0 0 0-2.83-2.83l-1.25 1.25a.751.751 0 0 1-1.042-.018.751.751 0 0 1-.018-1.042Zm-4.69 9.64a1.998 1.998 0 0 0 2.83 0l1.25-1.25a.751.751 0 0 1 1.042.018.751.751 0 0 1 .018 1.042l-1.25 1.25a3.5 3.5 0 1 1-4.95-4.95l2.5-2.5a3.5 3.5 0 0 1 4.95 0 .751.751 0 0 1-.018 1.042.751.751 0 0 1-1.042.018 1.998 1.998 0 0 0-2.83 0l-2.5 2.5a1.998 1.998 0 0 0 0 2.83Z"&gt;&lt;/path&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&#10; &lt;/a&gt;&#10;&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;ul&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;The result of &lt;code&gt;15 + 25&lt;/code&gt; is &lt;code&gt;40&lt;/code&gt;, which is still an untyped integer constant.&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;The result of &lt;code&gt;40 + 5.2&lt;/code&gt; is &lt;code&gt;45.2&lt;/code&gt;. Since &lt;code&gt;5.2&lt;/code&gt; is a floating-point constant, the entire expression&amp;rsquo;s result is inferred as &lt;code&gt;float64&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;/ul&gt;&#10;&lt;h3 class="heading-element" id="assignment-and-output"&gt;&lt;span&gt;Assignment and Output&lt;/span&gt;&#10; &lt;a href="#assignment-and-output" class="heading-mark"&gt;&#10; &lt;svg class="octicon octicon-link" viewBox="0 0 16 16" version="1.1" width="16" height="16" aria-hidden="true"&gt;&lt;path d="m7.775 3.275 1.25-1.25a3.5 3.5 0 1 1 4.95 4.95l-2.5 2.5a3.5 3.5 0 0 1-4.95 0 .751.751 0 0 1 .018-1.042.751.751 0 0 1 1.042-.018 1.998 1.998 0 0 0 2.83 0l2.5-2.5a2.002 2.002 0 0 0-2.83-2.83l-1.25 1.25a.751.751 0 0 1-1.042-.018.751.751 0 0 1-.018-1.042Zm-4.69 9.64a1.998 1.998 0 0 0 2.83 0l1.25-1.25a.751.751 0 0 1 1.042.018.751.751 0 0 1 .018 1.042l-1.25 1.25a3.5 3.5 0 1 1-4.95-4.95l2.5-2.5a3.5 3.5 0 0 1 4.95 0 .751.751 0 0 1-.018 1.042.751.751 0 0 1-1.042.018 1.998 1.998 0 0 0-2.83 0l-2.5 2.5a1.998 1.998 0 0 0 0 2.83Z"&gt;&lt;/path&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&#10; &lt;/a&gt;&#10;&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;ul&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;The final result &lt;code&gt;45.2&lt;/code&gt; is assigned to the variable &lt;code&gt;ans&lt;/code&gt;, whose type is &lt;code&gt;float64&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;&lt;code&gt;fmt.Println(ans)&lt;/code&gt; outputs &lt;code&gt;45.2&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;/ul&gt;&#10;&lt;/details&gt;</description></item><item><title>Go Daily Question 160</title><link>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_160/</link><pubDate>Mon, 13 Jan 2025 12:22:18 +0800</pubDate><guid>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_160/</guid><category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/godailyquestion/">GoDailyQuestion</category><category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/golanguagebasics/">GoLanguageBasics</category><description>&lt;p&gt;Will they compile? Why?&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;// 32-bit machine&#10;1）var x int32 = 32.&#10;2）var y int = x&#10;3）var z rune = x&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#10;&lt;details&gt;&#10; &lt;summary&gt;🔑 Answer Analysis:&lt;/summary&gt;&#10; &lt;p&gt;1 &lt;a href="https://go.dev/play/p/9eGpGYkcqqp" target="_blank" rel="external nofollow noopener noreferrer"&gt;Can compile&lt;/a&gt;&#10;2 &lt;a href="https://go.dev/play/p/L5LsYs4NvDO" target="_blank" rel="external nofollow noopener noreferrer"&gt;Cannot compile&lt;/a&gt;&#10;3 &lt;a href="https://go.dev/play/p/xgIjiu6PQ1l" target="_blank" rel="external nofollow noopener noreferrer"&gt;Can compile&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;h2 class="heading-element" id="analysis"&gt;&lt;span&gt;Analysis&lt;/span&gt;&#10; &lt;a href="#analysis" class="heading-mark"&gt;&#10; &lt;svg class="octicon octicon-link" viewBox="0 0 16 16" version="1.1" width="16" height="16" aria-hidden="true"&gt;&lt;path d="m7.775 3.275 1.25-1.25a3.5 3.5 0 1 1 4.95 4.95l-2.5 2.5a3.5 3.5 0 0 1-4.95 0 .751.751 0 0 1 .018-1.042.751.751 0 0 1 1.042-.018 1.998 1.998 0 0 0 2.83 0l2.5-2.5a2.002 2.002 0 0 0-2.83-2.83l-1.25 1.25a.751.751 0 0 1-1.042-.018.751.751 0 0 1-.018-1.042Zm-4.69 9.64a1.998 1.998 0 0 0 2.83 0l1.25-1.25a.751.751 0 0 1 1.042.018.751.751 0 0 1 .018 1.042l-1.25 1.25a3.5 3.5 0 1 1-4.95-4.95l2.5-2.5a3.5 3.5 0 0 1 4.95 0 .751.751 0 0 1-.018 1.042.751.751 0 0 1-1.042.018 1.998 1.998 0 0 0-2.83 0l-2.5 2.5a1.998 1.998 0 0 0 0 2.83Z"&gt;&lt;/path&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&#10; &lt;/a&gt;&#10;&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;h1 class="heading-element" id="go-language-type-system-and-code-analysis"&gt;&lt;span&gt;Go Language Type System and Code Analysis&lt;/span&gt;&#10; &lt;a href="#go-language-type-system-and-code-analysis" class="heading-mark"&gt;&#10; &lt;svg class="octicon octicon-link" viewBox="0 0 16 16" version="1.1" width="16" height="16" aria-hidden="true"&gt;&lt;path d="m7.775 3.275 1.25-1.25a3.5 3.5 0 1 1 4.95 4.95l-2.5 2.5a3.5 3.5 0 0 1-4.95 0 .751.751 0 0 1 .018-1.042.751.751 0 0 1 1.042-.018 1.998 1.998 0 0 0 2.83 0l2.5-2.5a2.002 2.002 0 0 0-2.83-2.83l-1.25 1.25a.751.751 0 0 1-1.042-.018.751.751 0 0 1-.018-1.042Zm-4.69 9.64a1.998 1.998 0 0 0 2.83 0l1.25-1.25a.751.751 0 0 1 1.042.018.751.751 0 0 1 .018 1.042l-1.25 1.25a3.5 3.5 0 1 1-4.95-4.95l2.5-2.5a3.5 3.5 0 0 1 4.95 0 .751.751 0 0 1-.018 1.042.751.751 0 0 1-1.042.018 1.998 1.998 0 0 0-2.83 0l-2.5 2.5a1.998 1.998 0 0 0 0 2.83Z"&gt;&lt;/path&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&#10; &lt;/a&gt;&#10;&lt;/h1&gt;&lt;h2 class="heading-element" id="1-untyped-literals"&gt;&lt;span&gt;1. Untyped Literals&lt;/span&gt;&#10; &lt;a href="#1-untyped-literals" class="heading-mark"&gt;&#10; &lt;svg class="octicon octicon-link" viewBox="0 0 16 16" version="1.1" width="16" height="16" aria-hidden="true"&gt;&lt;path d="m7.775 3.275 1.25-1.25a3.5 3.5 0 1 1 4.95 4.95l-2.5 2.5a3.5 3.5 0 0 1-4.95 0 .751.751 0 0 1 .018-1.042.751.751 0 0 1 1.042-.018 1.998 1.998 0 0 0 2.83 0l2.5-2.5a2.002 2.002 0 0 0-2.83-2.83l-1.25 1.25a.751.751 0 0 1-1.042-.018.751.751 0 0 1-.018-1.042Zm-4.69 9.64a1.998 1.998 0 0 0 2.83 0l1.25-1.25a.751.751 0 0 1 1.042.018.751.751 0 0 1 .018 1.042l-1.25 1.25a3.5 3.5 0 1 1-4.95-4.95l2.5-2.5a3.5 3.5 0 0 1 4.95 0 .751.751 0 0 1-.018 1.042.751.751 0 0 1-1.042.018 1.998 1.998 0 0 0-2.83 0l-2.5 2.5a1.998 1.998 0 0 0 0 2.83Z"&gt;&lt;/path&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&#10; &lt;/a&gt;&#10;&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;In Go, literals (such as &lt;code&gt;32.0&lt;/code&gt;) are untyped. Untyped literals can be implicitly inferred to the target type based on context.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Go Daily Question 134</title><link>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_134/</link><pubDate>Tue, 17 Dec 2024 14:00:22 +0800</pubDate><guid>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_134/</guid><category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/godailyquestion/">GoDailyQuestion</category><category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/golanguagebasics/">GoLanguageBasics</category><description>&lt;p&gt;What is the output of the following code?&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;func main() { &#10; a := 5&#10; b := 8.1&#10; fmt.Println(a &amp;#43; b)&#10;}&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;ul&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;A.13.1&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;B.13&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;C.compilation error&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;/ul&gt;&#10;&#10;&lt;details&gt;&#10; &lt;summary&gt;🔑 Answer Analysis:&lt;/summary&gt;&#10; &lt;p&gt;Answer: C. Run online: &lt;a href="https://go.dev/play/p/VDTgpXoukTb" target="_blank" rel="external nofollow noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://go.dev/play/p/VDTgpXoukTb&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;In Go, there is no implicit type conversion between variables. However, the compiler can perform implicit type conversion between variables and constants.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;In the problem, &lt;code&gt;a&lt;/code&gt; is an &lt;code&gt;int&lt;/code&gt; variable and &lt;code&gt;b&lt;/code&gt; is a &lt;code&gt;float64&lt;/code&gt; variable.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Go Daily Question 125</title><link>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_125/</link><pubDate>Sun, 08 Dec 2024 21:11:41 +0800</pubDate><guid>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_125/</guid><category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/godailyquestion/">GoDailyQuestion</category><category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/golanguagebasics/">GoLanguageBasics</category><description>&lt;p&gt;Today, here are two similar problems; note that more than half of people might get them wrong!&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;Problem 1:&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;package main&#10;&#10;func main() {&#10; var a int8 = -1&#10; var b int8 = -128 / a&#10;&#10; println(b)&#10;}&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;p&gt;Problem 2:&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;package main&#10;&#10;func main() {&#10; const a int8 = -1&#10; var b int8 = -128 / a&#10;&#10; println(b)&#10;}&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;p&gt;Will the above two problems compile? If they compile, what will be the output?&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Go Daily Question 091</title><link>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_091/</link><pubDate>Fri, 06 Dec 2024 20:45:18 +0800</pubDate><guid>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_091/</guid><category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/godailyquestion/">GoDailyQuestion</category><category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/golanguagebasics/">GoLanguageBasics</category><description>&lt;p&gt;What is the output of the following code?&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;const (&#10; a = iota&#10; b = iota&#10;)&#10;const (&#10; name = &amp;#34;name&amp;#34;&#10; c = iota&#10; d = iota&#10;)&#10;func main() {&#10; fmt.Println(a)&#10; fmt.Println(b)&#10; fmt.Println(c)&#10; fmt.Println(d)&#10;}&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#10;&lt;details&gt;&#10; &lt;summary&gt;🔑 Answer Analysis:&lt;/summary&gt;&#10; &lt;p&gt;Reference Answer and Analysis: 0 1 1 2.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;Key takeaway: Usage of iota.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;iota&lt;/code&gt; is Go language&amp;rsquo;s constant counter, and can only be used in constant expressions.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;iota&lt;/code&gt; is reset to 0 when the &lt;code&gt;const&lt;/code&gt; keyword appears, and each new line of constant declaration in a &lt;code&gt;const&lt;/code&gt; block increments &lt;code&gt;iota&lt;/code&gt; by one.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Go Daily Question 081</title><link>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_081/</link><pubDate>Fri, 06 Dec 2024 20:45:06 +0800</pubDate><guid>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_081/</guid><category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/godailyquestion/">GoDailyQuestion</category><category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/golanguagebasics/">GoLanguageBasics</category><description>&lt;p&gt;What is the output of the following code?&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;package main&#10;&#10;import (&#10; &amp;#34;fmt&amp;#34;&#10;)&#10;&#10;func main() {&#10; var ans float64 = 15 &amp;#43; 25 &amp;#43; 5.2&#10; fmt.Println(ans)&#10;}&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;p&gt;A: Cannot compile; B: 45; C: 45.2; D: 45.0&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&#10;&lt;details&gt;&#10; &lt;summary&gt;🔑 Answer Analysis:&lt;/summary&gt;&#10; &lt;p&gt;C&#10;Numbers are untyped constants.&#10;Or rather, they are arbitrary types.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;/details&gt;</description></item><item><title>Go Daily Question 033</title><link>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_033/</link><pubDate>Fri, 06 Dec 2024 20:44:09 +0800</pubDate><guid>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_033/</guid><category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/godailyquestion/">GoDailyQuestion</category><category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/golanguagebasics/">GoLanguageBasics</category><description>&lt;p&gt;Today, here are two similar problems; note that more than half of people might get them wrong!&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;package main&#10;&#10;func main() {&#10; var a int8 = -1&#10; var b int8 = -128 / a&#10;&#10; println(b)&#10;}&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;package main&#10;&#10;func main() {&#10; const a int8 = -1&#10; var b int8 = -128 / a&#10;&#10; println(b)&#10;}&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#10;&lt;details&gt;&#10; &lt;summary&gt;🔑 Answer Analysis:&lt;/summary&gt;&#10; &lt;p&gt;Problem 1:&#10;Because &lt;code&gt;var b int8 = -128 / a&lt;/code&gt; is not a constant expression, the untyped constant -128 is implicitly converted to &lt;code&gt;int8&lt;/code&gt; type (i.e., the same type as &lt;code&gt;a&lt;/code&gt;). Therefore, the result of &lt;code&gt;-128 / a&lt;/code&gt; is of &lt;code&gt;int8&lt;/code&gt; type, and its value is 128, which is outside the range of &lt;code&gt;int8&lt;/code&gt;. Since the result is not a constant, overflow is allowed. The binary representation of 128 is 10000000, which is exactly the two&amp;rsquo;s complement of -128. So, the result of the first problem is -128.&#10;Problem 2:&#10;-128 and -1 are both &lt;code&gt;const&lt;/code&gt;s and are evaluated directly at compile time. The untyped -128 is implicitly converted to &lt;code&gt;int8&lt;/code&gt;, and the result is 128, which is still a &lt;code&gt;const&lt;/code&gt;. Constant conversion does not allow overflow, leading to a compilation error. The real difference lies in the value of the expression: Problem 1 is not a constant, while Problem 2 is. Constant type conversion does not allow overflow followed by truncation.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Go Daily Question 028</title><link>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_028/</link><pubDate>Fri, 06 Dec 2024 19:49:55 +0800</pubDate><guid>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_028/</guid><category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/godailyquestion/">GoDailyQuestion</category><category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/golanguagebasics/">GoLanguageBasics</category><description>&lt;p&gt;Will the following code compile? If so, what will be the output?&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;const (&#10; x = iota&#10; _&#10; y&#10; z = &amp;#34;zz&amp;#34;&#10; k &#10; p = iota&#10;)&#10;&#10;func main() {&#10; fmt.Println(x,y,z,k,p)&#10;}&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#10;&lt;details&gt;&#10; &lt;summary&gt;🔑 Answer Analysis:&lt;/summary&gt;&#10; &lt;p&gt;Reference Answer: Compiles and outputs: 0 2 zz zz 5&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;When defining &lt;code&gt;const&lt;/code&gt;, if a variable is not followed by a value, it will use the value of the previous variable.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;const (&#10; x = iota&#10; _ = iota&#10; y = iota&#10; z = &amp;#34;zz&amp;#34;&#10; k = &amp;#34;zz&amp;#34;&#10; p = iota&#10;)&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;iota&lt;/code&gt; is equivalent to the line index of the &lt;code&gt;const&lt;/code&gt; block.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Go Daily Question 016</title><link>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_016/</link><pubDate>Fri, 06 Dec 2024 18:35:20 +0800</pubDate><guid>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_016/</guid><category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/godailyquestion/">GoDailyQuestion</category><category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/golanguagebasics/">GoLanguageBasics</category><description>&lt;p&gt;The author of Go101 published an interesting problem that primarily tests subtle details, a characteristic for which the book Go101 is well-known. Let&amp;rsquo;s look at this problem: what is the output of the following program? (Single choice)&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;package main&#10;&#10;const s = &amp;#34;Go101.org&amp;#34;&#10;&#10;// len(s) == 9&#10;// 1 &amp;lt;&amp;lt; 9 == 512&#10;// 512 / 128 == 4&#10;&#10;var a byte = 1 &amp;lt;&amp;lt; len(s) / 128&#10;var b byte = 1 &amp;lt;&amp;lt; len(s[:]) / 128&#10;&#10;func main() {&#10; println(a, b)&#10;}&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;ul&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;A、0 0&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;B、0 4&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;C：4 0&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;D：4 4&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;/ul&gt;&#10;&#10;&lt;details&gt;&#10; &lt;summary&gt;🔑 Answer Analysis:&lt;/summary&gt;&#10; &lt;p&gt;There are 2 key points here:&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Go Daily Question 018</title><link>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_018/</link><pubDate>Fri, 06 Dec 2024 18:34:52 +0800</pubDate><guid>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_018/</guid><category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/godailyquestion/">GoDailyQuestion</category><category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/golanguagebasics/">GoLanguageBasics</category><description>&lt;p&gt;What&amp;rsquo;s wrong with the following code?&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;package main&#10;&#10;const cl = 100&#10;&#10;var bl = 123&#10;&#10;func main() {&#10; println(&amp;amp;bl,bl)&#10; println(&amp;amp;cl,cl)&#10;}&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#10;&lt;details&gt;&#10; &lt;summary&gt;🔑 Answer Analysis:&lt;/summary&gt;&#10; &lt;p&gt;Answer: It won&amp;rsquo;t compile.&#10;&lt;code&gt;cl&lt;/code&gt; is a constant. The address of a constant cannot be taken.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;/details&gt;</description></item></channel></rss>